               fx[k] += dx*inv_dist3;\n           }\n       }\n       for (int i=start;i<end;++i){ p[i].vx += dt*fx[i-start]; ...}\n   }\n}\n\nBut note: This uses #pragma omp simd on inner loop over tile bodies. That may vectorize across the tile, but floating point reduction order might change? The compiler with -O3 and OpenMP SIMD may reassociate reductions if not careful. To preserve exact bitwise results, we should avoid auto-vectorization that changes summation order per i. However, here each fx[k] is accumulated independently; vectorizing across k does not change the order of j additions for a given k because each k has its own accumulator and the loop over j is sequential. The inner simd loop processes multiple k simultaneously but each k's accumulation sequence remains same (j outer). So bitwise results should be identical to scalar per i? Actually if we vectorize across k, the operations on different k are independent; for a given k, the addition order is still j=0..n-1. So yes.\n\nBut there is subtlety: The original code computes dx = p[j].x - p[i].x etc., then sqrd_dist = ... + SOFTENING, inv_dist = 1/sqrt(sqrd_dist), inv_dist3 = inv_dist*inv_dist*inv_dist, fx += dx * inv_dist3. If we compute xj once and px[k], same operations. Should be identical.\n\nHowever, the compiler might use FMA or different rounding if it reassociates? With -O3, GCC may contract floating point expressions (e.g., a*b+c) into fma unless -ffp-contract=off. The baseline compiled with default flags likely also uses contraction; but our code structure is similar so should produce same results. But to be safe, we can keep the exact expression order.\n\nWe need ensure that OpenMP parallelization over tiles does not change anything else. Also note: The original body_force updates velocities after computing forces for all i in one pass (i loop). In our version, each tile updates its own bodies' velocities independently; since tiles are disjoint and no cross-tile dependencies (forces only depend on positions which don't change during force computation), this is fine. Order of velocity updates across different i doesn't matter because they are independent.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: The original code computes forces for all i first, then updates velocities in the same loop? Actually it does both inside body_force: after inner j loop, it updates p[i].vx etc. So each i is updated immediately after its force sum completes. In our tile version, we compute forces for all i in tile (accumulate), then update velocities after finishing j loop. That's fine because velocity update doesn't affect other bodies' forces within the same iteration.\n\nSo correctness should hold.\n\nNow performance: We need to choose TILE size. For cache blocking, typical values 64 or 128. Since we have 4 cores, each thread processes multiple tiles; static schedule may cause load imbalance if n not divisible by tile count? But with many tiles (n/TILE), it's fine.\n\nWe also might consider using OpenMP collapse? Not needed.\n\nAnother improvement: Use restrict on pointers to help compiler. Also maybe use float arrays for positions of j bodies loaded into registers? The inner loop over ii reads p[j] once per tile, so memory traffic reduced.\n\nBut we must be careful with VLA initialization error from previous failed experiment (5f6ac2dcead441ce) where they used TILE as variable and initialized arrays. We'll use #define TILE 64 constant.\n\nAlso note: The baseline uses struct Body; we keep that layout for input/output compatibility. SoA would require conversion, but not necessary.\n\nLet's also consider using OpenMP reduction? Not needed.\n\nWe should also ensure the program compiles with -fopenmp? The Makefile likely already has flags? We don't control Makefile; only source file. But previous experiments used OpenMP and compiled fine, so compiler flags probably include -fopenmp or default? Actually we cannot change Makefile, but if baseline didn't use OpenMP, adding #pragma omp might require linking with libgomp. The build system likely compiles with gcc and maybe includes -fopenmp by default? We don't know. But previous experiments that used OpenMP were valid (e.g., 704fffe5d2a849b5 etc.), so it's allowed. So we can use OpenMP.\n\nWe need propose one candidate. Let's write the code with tiling and OpenMP parallel over tiles, plus maybe #pragma omp simd on inner loop to encourage vectorization across tile bodies (which is independent). Also add restrict qualifiers? Not necessary but could help.\n\nLet's craft src/nbody.c:\n\n- Include <omp.h>? Not needed if using pragmas.\n- Define TILE 64.\n- In body_force, use OpenMP parallel for over tiles.\n- Use local arrays px[TILE], py[TILE], pz[TILE] to hold positions of tile bodies (to avoid repeated reads from p[i] inside j loop).\n- Initialize fx/fy/fz arrays to zero.\n- Loop j outer, then inner ii over tile. Compute dx etc using xj and px[k].\n- After j loop, update velocities for tile.\n\nWe must ensure that the number of tiles is computed correctly: int n_tiles = (n + TILE - 1) / TILE; but if n=0? Not possible likely.\n\nAlso note: The original body_force uses float dt parameter. We'll keep same signature.\n\nPotential issue: If n < TILE, then only one tile with end=n. Works.\n\nWe should also consider that the inner loop over ii might be vectorized by compiler even without simd pragma; but adding #pragma omp simd may help. However, if we use OpenMP parallel for on tiles, each thread executes a tile sequentially; inside it has an inner loop over j and then inner loop over ii. The order of operations: For each tile, we iterate j from 0..n-1, and for each j we update all i in tile. This means that the accumulation for each i is done in increasing j order, same as original.\n\nBut note: In original, for a given i, it reads p[i] once per j? Actually it computes dx = p[j].x - p[i].x; so p[i] is read n times (once per j). Our version loads px[k] into local array once per tile, then uses that in inner loop. That's better.\n\nAlso we might want to use restrict on p pointer: void body_force(Body *restrict p, float dt, int n) but not necessary.\n\nLet's write the code carefully.\n\nWe also need to ensure no race conditions: Each thread writes only its own tile bodies' velocities and forces local arrays. No shared writes except reading p[j] which is read-only during force computation (positions don't change). So safe.\n\nOne more optimization: We can use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) on tiles; but if n_tiles < number of threads, some threads idle. For large N likely many tiles.\n\nLet's also consider using collapse? Not needed.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON with files array containing src/nbody.c content. Also hypothesis and transformations list.\n\nWe should mention that we preserve exact summation order per body by keeping j loop sequential within each tile, and parallelize over disjoint tiles.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n\n/*\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\n */\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n#define TILE 64\n\n/*\n * Compute the gravitational impact among all pairs of bodies in \n * the system.\n */\nvoid body_force(Body *p, float dt, int n) {\n  const int n_tiles = (n + TILE - 1) / TILE;\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int t = 0; t < n_tiles; ++t) {\n    const int start = t * TILE;\n    const int end = (start + TILE < n) ? start + TILE : n;\n    float px[TILE], py[TILE], pz[TILE];\n    for (int i = start; i < end; ++i) {\n      const int k = i - start;\n      px[k] = p[i].x;\n      py[k] = p[i].y;\n      pz[k] = p[i].z;\n    }\n    float fx[TILE] = {0.0f};\n    float fy[TILE] = {0.0f};\n    float fz[TILE] = {0.0f};\n\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      const float xj = p[j].x;\n      const float yj = p[j].y;\n      const float zj = p[j].z;\n      #pragma omp simd\n      for (int i = start; i < end; ++i) {\n        const int k = i - start;\n        const float dx = xj - px[k];\n        const float dy = yj - py[k];\n        const float dz = zj - pz[k];\n        const float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n        const float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n        const float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n        fx[k] += dx * inv_dist3;\n        fy[k] += dy * inv_dist3;\n        fz[k] += dz * inv_dist3;\n      }\n    }\n\n    for (int i = start; i < end; ++i) {\n      const int k = i - start;\n      p[i].vx += dt*fx[k];\n      p[i].vy += dt*fy[k];\n      p[i].vz += dt*fz[k];\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut note: The original code uses `1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist)` where sqrd_dist is float; in C, `sqrt` returns double unless using sqrtf. Original used `sqrt` (double) and then assigned to float inv_dist? Actually original: `float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);` Here `sqrd_dist` is float, but `sqrt` takes double and returns double; the division `1 / sqrt(...)` yields double, then converted to float. Our code uses `1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist)`. That might change rounding? To be safe, we should use exactly same expression: `float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);` but note that `sqrt` is from math.h and returns double; the division of int 1 by double gives double. In our code, if we write `1.0f / sqrt(...)`, it's float divided by double? Actually `1.0f` is float, `sqrt` returns double, so result is double then converted to float. Same as original? Original: `1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist)` – 1 is int, promoted to double because other operand double; result double assigned to float. So both produce same double value then cast to float. Should be identical.\n\nBut we used `const float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);` That's fine.\n\nHowever, there is a subtlety: The original